2026-10-15 13:38:38 BST | INFO     | nsflow:_log:821 - nsflow - 2026-10-15 13:38:38 - generated/hitl_document_redaction_obfuscation_network - INFO - {'otrace': ['orchestrator_supervisor_agent', 'ingestion_pagination_agent', 'classification_detection_agent', 'redaction_rendering_agent'], 'text': 'result from hitl_gateway_agent\n**Answer (I am the HITL gate — this is directly my scope):**\n\n**1. Does the HITL gate require per-page units from ingestion/pagination before it can build the review payload?**\nYes. Payload assembly is strictly *one page at a time* and is deterministic. To build the side-by-side review payload for a page I require the per-page unit as input — specifically `page_id`, `page_number`, and `total_pages`, together with that page\'s unredacted text + highlights and the corresponding redacted (masked) view. Without the per-page unit delivered by ingestion/pagination, there is nothing to assemble a review payload around. So per-page units are a hard precondition (an upstream dependency), not something the gate itself produces.\n\n**2. Is the ingestion/pagination determination itself in scope for the HITL gate, or elsewhere?**\nIt is **elsewhere** — outside my scope. The HITL gate does not perform detection, redaction, or learning, and it does **not** perform ingestion or pagination. Segmenting the source into page units is an upstream responsibility owned by the ingestion/pagination stage. My role begins *after* that: I consume the resulting per-page unit, present the review payload for that single page, and suspend in `WAITING_FOR_HUMAN` until an operator decides.\n\n**Summary of the boundary:**\n- Ingestion/pagination (producing per-page units) → **upstream, not mine**.\n- Review-payload assembly + human gate + decision capture → **mine (HITL gate)**.\n- Feedback records for ADD/REMOVE edits → **downstream feedback-logging stage** (which confirmed it is not the owner of this question).\n\nBecause more than one component touches this inquiry and the payload/gate portion is squarely mine while the ingestion/pagination portion is not, the clean split above is the accurate clarification. If you want a definitive ruling on *which* upstream component owns ingestion/pagination, that agent should be consulted directly — I can only confirm it is not the HITL gate.\n\n```json\n{\n    "Name": "HITL Gate (Redaction Review)",\n    "Inquiry": "Clarify: for the customer support & DSAR log redaction task, does the HITL gate require per-page units produced by ingestion/pagination before it can build the review payload, and is the ingestion/pagination determination itself in scope for the HITL gate or elsewhere?",\n    "Mode": "Fulfill",\n    "Response": "Yes to the first part, no to the second. (1) The HITL gate requires per-page units from ingestion/pagination as a hard precondition: payload assembly is deterministic and executed one page at a time, and needs page_id, page_number, total_pages plus the unredacted text/highlights and the masked text_with_masks for that page. Without the upstream per-page unit there is nothing to assemble. (2) The ingestion/pagination determination is NOT in scope for the HITL gate. The HITL gate does not perform ingestion, pagination, detection, redaction, or learning; ingestion/pagination is owned upstream (the ingestion/pagination component). The gate only consumes the resulting per-page unit, presents the side-by-side review payload, halts in WAITING_FOR_HUMAN with no auto-advance, and captures the explicit human decision into the approval_matrix keyed by page_id. The downstream feedback-logging stage was consulted and confirmed it is not the owner of this inquiry; the ADD/REMOVE edit routing remains its role."\n}\n```'}
