2026-10-15 13:38:48 BST | INFO     | nsflow:_log:821 - nsflow - 2026-10-15 13:38:48 - generated/hitl_document_redaction_obfuscation_network - INFO - {'otrace': ['orchestrator_supervisor_agent', 'hitl_gateway_agent'], 'text': 'result from feedback_logging_agent\nThe learning agent confirmed it cannot own the dataset-scoping question (that sits with the gateway\'s configuration) but specified its downstream-consumer requirements. Here is the compiled follow-up response:\n\n```json\n{\n    "Name": "Feedback Logging Agent",\n    "Inquiry": "Determine whether the HITL review gateway applies to the redaction of the customer support & DSAR request log test dataset. If it applies, what do you require from the gateway to log edits?",\n    "Mode": "Fulfill",\n    "Response": "1) APPLICABILITY: Dataset-scoping of the HITL review gateway (i.e., whether it is enabled for the customer support & DSAR request log test dataset) is a routing/policy decision owned by the gateway configuration — both the logging stage and the learning stage confirmed they do not set it. From the logging stage, applicability is purely event-based: the pipeline applies to this dataset exactly when an operator issues a MODIFY (a span is added or removed) or a REJECT at the page review gate for its pages. If the dataset\'s pages are human-reviewed, every one of those edits is logged; test-dataset reviews are logged identically to production reviews (append-only, no dedupe) and remain traceable to the dataset. If no human edits occur, nothing reaches the logging stage and there is nothing to log.\\n\\n2) WHAT I REQUIRE FROM THE GATEWAY PER EDIT — exactly one structured object per edit with these fields:\\n- page_id: deterministic identifier of the reviewed page (should carry or embed the dataset identifier so records are attributable to the customer support & DSAR request log test dataset).\\n- change_type: ADD (reviewer added a span the classifier missed) or REMOVE (reviewer removed/rejected a classifier-flagged span; a REJECT is logged as REMOVE of the flagged span).\\n- classifier_id: the classifier the edit implicates (for ADD, the proposed classifier).\\n- category: the sensitive-data subtype the span belongs to (e.g., the applicable PII/PHI subtype for support-ticket/DSAR content).\\n- context_snippet: surrounding text needed to interpret the span.\\n- span: exact character offsets/boundaries of the edit.\\n- confidence: reviewer- or system-supplied confidence.\\n- reviewer_id: identity of the human who made the edit.\\n- timestamp: ISO-8601 time the edit was made or logged.\\n\\n3) DELIVERY BEHAVIOR REQUIRED: forward each edit immediately on capture (no batching, deferral, or holding); never deduplicate, coalesce, or drop repeated, near-duplicate, or contradictory edits; preserve values exactly as captured (verbatim, no normalization of reviewer intent); send null for any genuinely unknown field rather than omitting the record or field; keep records machine-readable and schema-stable (one structured object per edit).\\n\\n4) DOWNSTREAM REQUIREMENTS (confirmed with the learning stage): the learning stage additionally requires the human decision type captured in structured form (APPROVE / MODIFY / REJECT), operator edits preserved verbatim, an affected span/classifier-rule reference, and dataset-level tagging — without it, error patterns cannot be attributed to this dataset.\\n\\n5) HANDSHAKE: each record is durably persisted append-only before accumulated structured feedback is forwarded to the learning stage; on write failure the failure is reported explicitly and retried, so the gateway should retain/re-transmit an edit until persistence is confirmed. No stage applies classifier updates or treats a proposal as approved — proposals land in PENDING_ADMIN_REVIEW."\n}\n```'}
