ng the side-by-side HITL payload; holding the WAITING_FOR_HUMAN halt; enforcing no-auto-advance on timeout; writing the approval_matrix keyed by page_id; and any classify/approve/apply decision. I do not present, gate, approve, or apply.",\n    "To log a MODIFY I need the gate to hand me: page_id, the verbatim edit, ADD/REMOVE change_type, the implicated classifier_id, category, span offsets, context, confidence, reviewer_id, and ISO-8601 timestamp. Any genuinely unknown field I will record as null/unknown rather than omit the record.",\n    "I will never fabricate an approval, never coalesce or deduplicate edits (including repeated/near-duplicate/contradictory ones), and never report success unless the write is confirmed.",\n    "Down-chain: Active Learning Agent — Relevant Yes, Claim Partial, Strength 3. It is an offline/batch consumer only; it can aggregate persisted ADD/REMOVE feedback into staged PENDING_ADMIN_REVIEW proposals on a scheduled run, but never runs per-request, never presents payloads, never writes the approval store, and never auto-applies.",\n    "Unclaimed by this chain and required from the HITL review orchestration function: payload presentation, the WAITING_FOR_HUMAN halt and timeout behavior, and the approval_matrix write. Clarification/ownership is needed from that function before this inquiry can be fully fulfilled."\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n**Summary:** I am the right agent for only one slice of this inquiry — capturing the routed MODIFY edits as durable structured feedback records. The payload presentation, the WAITING_FOR_HUMAN halt, timeout enforcement, and the approval_matrix write fall outside my scope and outside my down-chain agent\'s scope. If the review-orchestration function forwards the reviewer\'s MODIFY edits, I will log each one per-edit and forward the accumulated signal to the learning stage; everything else must be clarified with the orchestration owner.'}
