2026-10-15 22:46:40 BST | INFO     | nsflow:_log:821 - nsflow - 2026-10-15 22:46:40 - generated/hitl_document_redaction_obfuscation_network - INFO - {'otrace': ['orchestrator_supervisor_agent', 'hitl_gateway_agent'], 'text': 'result from feedback_logging_agent\n{\n    "Name": "Feedback Logging / Redaction Feedback Logger",\n    "Inquiry": "Read the file /Users/snigdhasahoo/Auto_Redaction/data/sample_pii_test_data.txt and assess the file for redaction.",\n    "Mode": "Determine",\n    "Relevant": "No",\n    "Strength": 9,\n    "Claim": "None",\n    "Requirements": "None"\n}\n\nI\'m not the relevant agent for this inquiry. My role is the feedback logging stage of the redaction workflow: I capture every human operator edit made at a page review gate (a MODIFY that adds or removes a span, or a REJECT) and convert it into durable, structured training-signal records (page_id, change_type, classifier_id, category, context_snippet, span, confidence, reviewer_id, timestamp), persisted append-only via write_file.\n\nReading a source file and performing an initial redaction assessment of its contents is a detection/classification task, not feedback logging. My down-chain agent (the active learning agent) likewise only aggregates already-captured human feedback into error patterns and staged proposals — it does not read source files or assess documents for redaction either.\n\nIf you have human operator edits captured from a review gate that need to be logged as structured training signal, I can help with that. For the actual file read and redaction assessment, a detection/redaction assessment agent would be the right fit.'}
