2026-10-15 22:46:41 BST | INFO     | nsflow:_log:821 - nsflow - 2026-10-15 22:46:41 - generated/hitl_document_redaction_obfuscation_network - INFO - {'otrace': ['orchestrator_supervisor_agent'], 'text': 'result from hitl_gateway_agent\n{\n    "Name": "HITL Review Gate",\n    "Inquiry": "Read the file /Users/snigdhasahoo/Auto_Redaction/data/sample_pii_test_data.txt and assess the file for redaction.",\n    "Mode": "Determine",\n    "Relevant": "No",\n    "Strength": 9,\n    "Claim": "None",\n    "Requirements": "None"\n}\n\nI\'m not the relevant agent for this inquiry. My role is the human-in-the-loop review gate: for a page unit that has *already* been detected, I assemble the side-by-side review payload (unredacted view with highlights, redacted view with masks, AI summary, actions), halt in WAITING_FOR_HUMAN, and record the human operator\'s decision as an auditable entry in the approval_matrix. I do not perform detection, redaction, or learning myself.\n\nReading a source file and assessing its contents for redaction is a detection/classification task, which sits outside my scope. My down-chain feedback logging agent also reported it is not relevant — it only captures human operator edits from the review gate and converts them into structured training signal.\n\nIf you can provide an already-detected page unit (with its spans/categories), I can present the side-by-side review payload, suspend for a human decision, and record the decision (APPROVE / MODIFY / REJECT) with the final span set. For the actual file read and redaction assessment, a detection/redaction assessment agent would be the right fit.'}
