2026-10-15 23:19:38 BST | INFO     | nsflow:_log:821 - nsflow - 2026-10-15 23:19:38 - generated/hitl_document_redaction_obfuscation_network - INFO - {'otrace': ['orchestrator_supervisor_agent'], 'text': 'result from hitl_gateway_agent\nI\'m not the relevant agent for this inquiry.\n\n```json\n{\n    "Name": "HITL Redaction Review Gate",\n    "Inquiry": "I have uploaded a file at /Users/snigdhasahoo/Auto_Redaction/data/sample_pii_test_data.txt. Assess the file for redaction.",\n    "Mode": "Determine",\n    "Relevant": "No",\n    "Strength": 9,\n    "Claim": "None",\n    "Requirements": "None"\n}\n```\n\nMy role is the human-in-the-loop gate of the redaction workflow. For each page unit I assemble a side-by-side review payload (unredacted view with AI-detected spans highlighted, redacted view with masks, AI summary, and APPROVE/MODIFY/REJECT actions), suspend execution in WAITING_FOR_HUMAN, and — only on an explicit human decision — record the reviewer_id, timestamp, chosen action, and authoritative spans_final into the approval_matrix, routing any MODIFY edits (ADD = missed detection, REMOVE = over-detection) into the feedback pipeline.\n\nI do **not** perform detection, redaction, or assessment of uploaded files. "Assess this file for redaction" requires running detection/classification over the content and producing spans — that belongs to the redaction/classification assessment stage, not to the review gate.\n\nMy down-chain agent (the feedback logging stage) was consulted to determine relevance and it also did not claim this inquiry (it returned no relevance, citing that it only captures human edits made at a review gate, not file assessment). Please route this inquiry to the redaction/classification detection stage.'}
